modified versions, including static builds, public previews, mirrors, and
versions embedded in other websites. It applies whether access is free,
paid, commercial, educational, or otherwise noncommercial.

A service is publicly accessible if members of the public can access it,
including through registration, payment, or a shared password. An unlisted
URL alone does not make a deployment private. Publishing source code for
inspection or download as permitted by section 2 is not public hosting of
the running application.

